ng-log is a C++14 library for application-level logging.

It provides stream-based logging and helper macros.

ng-log is the community-maintained successor to glog, originally
developed by Google. The project, however, is not affiliated with
Google, which maintains its own logging library as part of abseil-cpp.

ng-log is a replacement for google-glog, which is no longer
maintained. By default it builds a glog compatibility layer
(libglog, <glog/*.h> headers and the glog CMake config), so software
that uses glog can build against it unchanged.

ng-log and google-glog are mutually exclusive. Remove google-glog
before installing this package, then rebuild any packages that were
linked against it (the compat libglog has a different soname).

Note: upstream deprecates the glog compatibility layer and will
remove it in ng-log 0.9.0. Packages using google-glog should move
to <ng-log/logging.h> and the ng-log CMake package (or libng-log
pkg-config) before then.

Options:

  COMPAT=no    build without the glog compatibility layer. ng-log can
               then be installed alongside google-glog.
  GFLAGS=yes   enable gflags support (requires gflags).

Example:

  GFLAGS=yes ./ng-log.SlackBuild
